Pluggable TOAST

I. What is Pluggable TOAST

TOAST is slicing and storing an oversized value externally to Tuple.
Currently TOAST is a part of Heap AM (Core). Not extensible, has
same strategy for all datatypes, and not effective for structured
data (json) or data required special workflow (bytea).

Pluggable TOAST propose storing a value outside Tuple, with means
and storage depending on a specific entity incapsulating all storage
mechanics and hiding it from Table AM which calls it. Pluggable TOAST
consists of TOAST API which allows calling custom value processing
routines, and the mentioned routines - the entities we call Toasters.

In short:
How TOAST Works
- When tuple size exceeds some internal limit (usually page size,
  because a tuple must fit to a page) - Heap AM decides to TOAST
  large attributes of a tuple;
- Attribute is stored in TOAST relation. Attribute value is replaced
  with Toast Pointer containing TOAST relation OID and value ID in
  TOAST table;
- 4 strategies depending on storage type;
- TOAST does not know anything about data being TOASTed;

How Pluggable TOAST Works
- When tuple size exceeds some internal limit Heap decides to TOAST
  large attributes. As an option we suggest special flag to force
  value to be TOASTed despite of its size;
- Table AM searches for implementation of TOAST according to OID
  assigned to table's column (the Toaster).
- Attribute is replaced with Custom Toast Pointer created by specific
  Toaster. Custom TOAST Pointer contains information required by
  specific Toaster to retrieve TOASTed data - Toaster OID, etc;
- TOAST and storage details are implemented in custom Toaster and
  are hidden from AM behind the Toaster;
- Toaster can use any knowledge of data structure and workflow;
- Same data could be TOASTed with different Toasters;

II. The Pluggable TOAST

Pluggable TOAST proposes an open API allowing to develop and plug in
custom Toasters for table column and data types. Toaster could process
TOASTed data using knowledge about internal data structure and workflow.

Toasters could use any storage, advanced compression, encryption, and
other functionality internally, without aware TAM that calls Toaster.

Pluggable TOAST consists of the core - the TOAST API, and custom Toasters
plugged into PostgreSQL with this API.

TOAST API does not possess any logic regarding TOAST functionality, it is
just a wraparound that allow to register, call and drop plugged Toasters.

To be able to use custom TOAST implementations the TOAST API defines new
type of TOAST pointer - in addition to External (varatt_external) and
Extended (varatt_extended) it defines Custom Toast Pointer as varatt_custom,
and adds macros to use with it alike to varatt_external.

This version of TOAST API uses hooks and SQL functions as interface instead
of heavy core modification. Custom TOAST implementations are called via hooks
in 'toastapi' extension. Thus, any custom Toaster requires toastapi extension
to be installed beforehand.

III. The Default and Pluggable TOAST

And how we'll look how the Pluggable TOAST works with the default TOAST.
The default TOAST mechanics uses a special kind of structure to substitute
the original value in the base table - the External TOAST Pointer. It is
defined by the header of the VARLENA:

typedef struct
{
	uint8		va_header;		/* Always 0x80 or 0x01 */
	uint8		va_tag;			/* Type of datum */
	char		va_data[FLEXIBLE_ARRAY_MEMBER]; /* Type-specific data */
} varattrib_1b_e;

Field VA_HEADER stands for regular VARLENA header and defines whether it is
TOAST pointer or other kind of data. For TOAST pointers field VA_TAG defines
kind of pointer - Ondisk, Indirect, Expanded and External and field VA_DATA
contains the TOAST pointer data.
Current tags are:
	VARTAG_INDIRECT = 1,
	VARTAG_EXPANDED_RO = 2,
	VARTAG_EXPANDED_RW = 3,
	VARTAG_ONDISK = 18

We add here one more tag for new custom TOAST pointer structure:
	VARTAG_CUSTOM = 4

By using VARLENA structure we could use Custom TOAST pointers along with the
default ones, in the same tables, without affecting already-existing data.

The default TOAST pointer - the VARATT_EXTERNAL has the following structure:

typedef struct varatt_external
{
	int32		va_rawsize;		/* Original data size (includes header) */
	uint32		va_extinfo;		/* External saved size (without header) and
								 * compression method */
	Oid			va_valueid;		/* Unique ID of value within TOAST table */
	Oid			va_toastrelid;	/* RelID of TOAST table containing it */
}			varatt_external;

The Pluggable TOAST defines new kind of TOAST Pointer - the Custom Pointer.
The Custom Pointer has different internal structure from the defult one:

typedef struct varatt_custom
{
	uint32align16	va_toasterdatalen;/* total size of toast pointer, < BLCKSZ */
	uint32align16	va_rawsize;	/* Original data size (includes header) */
	uint32align16	va_toasterid;	/* Toaster ID, actually Oid */
	char		va_toasterdata[FLEXIBLE_ARRAY_MEMBER];	/* Custom toaster data */
}			varatt_custom;

The Custom TOAST pointer is used instead of External one, and unlike External
has extensible structure - it allows to use the same Custom pointer by different
Custom Toasters.

Because custom TOAST pointers could exist along with the default ones custom
Toasters could be safely assigned and de-assigned to regular tables. See p.VI
for examples.

IV. The Limitations of the Pluggable TOAST

Currently the Pluggable TOAST has several limitations:

1) Custom Toasters must be assigned only to a columns with the External storage
mode;

2) Custom TOAST pointers are not logically replicated because replication engine
require some improvements;

3) Custom Toasters are not restored from dumps, and require manual actions to be
restored and data to be TOASTed with custom Toasters;

4) When Custom Toaster is assigned to a table column all the following transactions
after the Toaster assignment data being stored into the TOAST tables is processed
with the Toaster assigned to a column.

5) When Custom Toaster is assigned to a table column old data values left as-is
and are not re-toasted. Although while being updated the old data value is TOASTed
with the assigned Toaster because of workflow of the default TOAST mechanics.

6) If the Toaster is somehow dropped the data toasted with it will be not
accessible by any standard means and considered to be lost. To protect TOASTed values
the API creates some dependencies in the system (PG_DEPEND table):
- when the Toaster is added to a system a Toaster-Extension dependency is created;
- when the Toaster is assigned to a column a Toaster-Relation dependency is created;
- when the Toaster is de-assigned from a column Toaster-Relation dependency is deleted;
- when the Toaster is dropped from a system Toaster-Extension dependency is deleted;

V. The TOAST API

TOAST API is a hook-based interface that allows to call custom TOAST
implementations. TOAST API consists of 2 parts:

1) PostgreSQL core patch that installs necessary hooks, new flag that
is used to distinguish custom TOAST pointers from regular ones and macros
wrapping this flag and size calculation for custom pointer;
2) toastapi extension that
   - defines custom toast pointer - varatt_custom;
   - creates special schema pgpro_toast containing table PG_TOASTER that
   holds all plugged in Toasters and is used by TOAST API internal services
   for Toasters handling;
   - contains hooks implementation (looking up for a Toaster if needed
   and calling corresponding function);
   - contains SQL interface for adding, dropping and assigning Toasters
   to a table columns.

Internal table PGPRO_TOAST.PG_TOASTER contains Toaster OID, name and handler
function:
select * from PG_TOASTER;
  oid  |    tsrname    |       tsrhandler
-------+---------------+-------------------------
  9864 | deftoaster    | default_toaster_handler
(1 rows)

Toasters assigned to a table columns are stored in columnd options -
attoptions field. TOAST API does this automatically. When custom TOAST
function is called TOAST API retrieves Toaster reference from attoptions
and calls corresponding Toaster.

Custom TOAST pointer
VARATT_CUSTOM is an extensible Custom TOAST pointer format:
typedef struct varatt_custom
{
	uint32align16	va_toasterdatalen;/* total size of toast pointer, < BLCKSZ */
	uint32align16	va_rawsize;		/* Original data size (includes header) */
	uint32align16	va_toasterid;	/* Toaster ID, actually Oid */
	char		va_toasterdata[FLEXIBLE_ARRAY_MEMBER];	/* Custom toaster data */
}			varatt_custom;

where fixed fields are required for TOAST wrapping functions, and flexible
member holds any custom data stored by a specific Toaster.

TOAST API installs the following hooks in PostgreSQL core:
The main TOAST entry point - calls Toaster toast function (mandatory) which
stores given value, returns TOAST pointer.
typedef Datum (*Toastapi_toast_hook_type) (Relation rel,
										   int attnum,
										   Datum value,
										   int max_length,
										   int am_options);

Update TOASTed value hook entry point - calls Toaster Update routine,
if it has one, and returns TOAST pointer returned.
typedef Datum (*Toastapi_update_hook_type) (Relation rel,
											int attnum,
											Datum new_value,
											Datum old_value,
											int am_options);

Copy TOASTed value hook entry point - calls Toaster routine responsible
for copying existing TOASTed object.
typedef Datum (*Toastapi_copy_hook_type) (Relation rel,
										  int attnum,
										  Datum value,
										  int am_options);

Delete TOASTed value hook entry point calls Toaster delete TOASTed value
function, if it has any. Does not return a value.
typedef void (*Toastapi_delete_hook_type) (Relation rel,
										   int attnum,
										   Datum value,
										   bool is_speculative);

Restore TOASTed value to it's original state entry point - the second
mandatory function any Toaster must implement. Returns restored value as
varlena Datum.
typedef Datum (*Toastapi_detoast_hook_type) (Datum value,
											 int offset,
											 int length);

This hook allows passing size calculation for Custom TOAST pointer to toastapi
extension, along with the definition of Custom Pointer itself, instead of
defining Custom pointer in the core.
typedef Size (*Toastapi_size_hook_type) (const void *ptr,
										 ToastPtrSizeType sz_type);

VI. The SQL Interface

TOAST API provides simple SQL interface for adding, assigning and dropping
Toasters:

add_toaster(toaster_name text, toaster_handler_func text)
- plug in new Toaster (added before as an extension). This function also creates
a dependency in PG_DEPEND for Toaster OID to its extension OID, which is used to
protect Toaster from being dropped by dropping the Toaster extension.
Input:
toaster_name text       - Toaster name to be stored in PG_TOASTER. Must be unique.
toaster_handler_func text - Toaster handler function name (automatically added as
                        a regproc when the Toaster extension is installed)
Output:
integer                 - OID assigned to a new Toaster

set_toaster(toaster_name text, tab_name text, col_name text)
- assign Toaster to a table column.  This function also creates a dependency in
PG_DEPEND for Toaster OID to the Relation OID. This dependency is used to protect
the TOASTed data by restricting to drop the Toaster if it is assigned to a column.
Input:
toaster_name text       - Name of Toaster assigned to a table column
tab_name text           - Table name
col_name text           - Table column name
Output:
integer                 - OID of a Toaster assigned to a column

reset_toaster(tab_name text, col_name text)
- reset TOAST mechanism to a default one for a table column. This function drops
Toaster-Relation dependency allowing to drop the Toaster later when all depencencies
will be dropped.
Input:
tab_name text           - Table name
col_name text           - Table column name
Output:
integer                 - always 0


drop_toaster(toaster_name text)
- drop Toaster from a system. Only unused Toasters that does not have any data
TOASTed with them could be dropped! This case is protected by the dependencies
created by add_toaster and set_toaster functions (see above). If a dependency
still exists Toaster would not be dropped.
Input:
toaster_name text       - Name of Toaster to be dropped
Output:
integer                 - dropped Toaster OID if success, 0 if Toaster is used

get_toaster(tab_name text, col_name text)
- get Toaster OID assigned to a table column
Input:
tab_name text           - Table name
col_name text           - Table column name
Output:
integer                 - OID of Toaster assigned to a Column, 0 if none

get_toaster_id(toaster_name text)
- get Toaster OID by toaster name;
Input:
toaster_name text		- Toaster name
Output:
integer					- OID of Toaster with given name, 0 if none

The example below shows usage flow for the Custom Toaster. Also it shows how the
TOASTed values are being protected by tracking dependencies:

-- create new Toaster extension
CREATE EXTENSION bytea_toaster;
CREATE EXTENSION
Time: 3.637 ms

-- add newly created Toaster to a system if it is not done by the Toaster initialization
SELECT add_toaster('bytea_toaster', 'bytea_toaster_handler');

-- create new table
CREATE TABLE t (id int, b bytea);
CREATE TABLE
Time: 8.504 ms

-- set storage mode = EXTERNAL to a column before assigning a Toaster
ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN b SET STORAGE external;
ALTER TABLE
Time: 3.563 ms

postgres@postgres=# INSERT into t values (1, repeat('a',3000)::bytea);
INSERT 0 1
Time: 7.077 ms

-- set Toaster to a table column
postgres@postgres=# SELECT pgpro_toast.set_toaster('bytea_toaster','t','b');
 set_toaster
-------------
       16408
(1 row)

postgres@postgres=# insert into t values (2, repeat('b',3000)::bytea);
INSERT 0 1
Time: 4.397 ms

-- unset Toaster to use the default one
postgres@postgres=# SELECT pgpro_toast.reset_toaster('t','b');
 reset_toaster
---------------
             0
(1 row)
-- drop existing Toaster from system
postgres@postgres=# SELECT pgpro_toast.drop_toaster('bytea_toaster');
 drop_toaster
--------------

(1 row)

postgres@postgres=# drop extension bytea_toaster;
ERROR:  cannot drop extension bytea_toaster because other objects depend on it
DETAIL:  table t depends on extension bytea_toaster
HINT:  Use DROP ... CASCADE to drop the dependent objects too.
Time: 0.682 ms

postgres@postgres=# drop extension bytea_toaster cascade;
ERROR:  cache lookup failed for function 16408
Time: 0.541 ms

postgres@postgres=# select * from t;
 id |                                                                                                                  >
----+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>
  1 | \x616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616161616>
  2 | \x626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626262626>
(2 rows)

Time: 0.822 ms

VII. The Toaster

Custom Toaster should be defined and included as an PostgreSQL Extension
via standard PostgreSQL Extension mechanics. Although any Custom Toaster
could be plugged in only with toastapi extension installed, so it should
either have
requires = 'toastapi'
directive in its control file, or be manually controlled to have toastapi
extension pre-installed.

Any custom Toaster should have a handler and pre-defined method set.
Toaster header must be defined according to toastapi.h and must have
several mandatory methods to be implemented to toast and detoast
(store and retrieve) data.

TOAST API interface is defined with TsrRoutine structure with pre-defined
set of methods (check contrib/toastapi/toastapi.h):
typedef struct TsrRoutine
{
	/* mandatory interface functions */
	toaster_validate_function tsr_validate;
	toaster_toast_function tsr_toast;
	toaster_detoast_function tsr_detoast;
	/* optional interface functions */
	toaster_update_function tsr_update;
	toaster_copy_function tsr_copy;
	toaster_delete_function tsr_delete;
} TsrRoutine;

TOAST API method description:

typedef Datum (*toaster_toast_function) (ToasterContext tcxt,
										 Datum value,
										 Datum old_value,
										 int max_inline_size,
										 int am_options,
										 char att_storage,
										 ToastCompressionId cmid);
- (!) mandatory function, TOASTs incoming value - actual external storage
is done here. Returns TOAST pointer referencing original value that was
TOASTed.

Input:
ToasterContext tcxt     - structure containing TOAST storage reference, Toaster OID,
                        TOASTed attribute number, TOAST options;
Datum value             - original value to be TOASTed;
Datum oldvalue          - TOASTed value in old TOAST table for rewrite case;
int max_inline_size     - maximum chunk size that could be stored directly in TOAST pointer;
int am_options          - access method options
char att_storage        - attribute STORAGE option
ToastCompressionId cmid - TOAST compression method ID
Output:
Datum - TOAST pointer structure referencing value stored externally.
This TOAST pointer is stored in original table instead of original data.

typedef Datum (*toaster_update_function) (ToasterContext tcxt,
										  Datum new_value,
										  Datum old_value,
										  int am_options);
Update TOASTed value. Returns TOAST pointer to an updated value;

Input:
ToasterContext tcxt     - structure containing TOAST storage reference, Toaster OID,
                        TOASTed attribute number, TOAST options;
Datum value             - TOAST pointer to value to be updated;
Datum oldvalue          - TOAST pointer to new value;
int am_options          - Access method options
Output:
Datum - TOAST pointer structure referencing updated value.

typedef Datum (*toaster_copy_function) (ToasterContext tcxt,
										Datum value,
										int am_options);
Create copy of an existing TOASTed value. Returns TOAST pointer to a new value.

Input:
ToasterContext tcxt     - structure containing TOAST storage reference, Toaster OID,
                        TOASTed attribute number, TOAST options;
Datum value             - TOASTed value to be copied;
int am_options          - Access method options
Output:
Datum - TOAST pointer structure referencing value stored externally.
This TOAST pointer is stored in given TOAST table instead of original data.

typedef Datum (*toaster_detoast_function) (ToasterContext tcxt,
										   Datum toast_ptr,
										   int offset,
										   int length);
- (!) mandatory function, recovers toasted data from external storage
into original data (regular varlena). Returns original value as Datum
casted from varlena.

Input:
ToasterContext tcxt     - structure containing TOAST storage reference, Toaster OID,
                        TOASTed attribute number, TOAST options;
Datum toast_ptr         - TOAST pointer referencing TOASTed value to recover;
int offset              - Data offset in original value if we have to recover not
                        all value but slice;
int length              - Slice length to recover;
Output:
Datum - original value recovered from TOAST table. For regular use
must be of varlena type, but casting to Datum allows to return pointer
to any kind of structure.

typedef void (*toaster_delete_function) (ToasterContext tcxt,
										 Datum value,
										 bool is_speculative);
- Deletes TOASTed value from TOAST storage. Does not return a value.
Input:
ToasterContext tcxt     - structure containing TOAST storage reference, Toaster OID,
                        TOASTed attribute number, TOAST options;
Datum value             - TOASTed value to delete;
bool is_speculative     - flag for tuple inserted by the same command that deletes it;
Output:
void

typedef bool (*toaster_validate_function) (Oid toasteroid, Oid typeoid,
										   char storage, char compression,
										   Oid amoid, bool false_ok);
- validates given data type for current Toaster.

Input:
Oid toasteroid          - Toaster OID to validate;
Oid typeoid             - Data type OID to validate;
char storage            - STORAGE parameter from table column, see SET STORAGE;
char compression        - COMPRESSION parameter from table column, see SET COMPRESSION;
Oid amoid               - Access method OID - is provided for future use, in case of
                        some other AM is used instead of Heap;
bool false_ok           - Sets validation method behavior in case of validation
                        has not passed - should it fail or just return false;
Output:
bool - is passed type valid for current Toaster (true) or not (false).

Please note: functions highlighted with "(!)" are mandatory and
must be implemented. Other functions are optional.

IIX. Extending the TOAST

An important part of the Pluggable TOAST API is its extensibility.
TOAST API provides an interface for using user-defined functions
in addition to pre-defined TOAST API functions set.

In addition to pre-defined functions like toast, detoast, etc.,
TOAST API provides special function "get_vtable":

typedef void * (*get_vtable_function) (Datum toast_ptr);

This function is intended to return virtual table of user-defined
functions which could be used by developer, for example adding
iterators to the default Toaster.

An example of get_vtable extended method set:
/* Virtual functions table in Generic Toaster */
static void *
generic_toaster_vtable(Datum toast_ptr)
{
	GenericToastRoutine *routine = palloc0(sizeof(*routine));

	routine->iterator_init = generic_iterator_init;
        routine->detoast_next = generic_detoast_iterate;

	return routine;
}

Datum
default_toaster_handler(PG_FUNCTION_ARGS)
{
	TsrRoutine *tsrroutine = makeNode(TsrRoutine);

        ...
	tsrroutine->get_vtable = generic_toaster_vtable;
        ...

	PG_RETURN_POINTER(tsrroutine);
}


/* Using virtual functions table */
TsrRoutine *toaster = SearchTsrCache(DEFAULT_TOASTER_OID);
GenericToastRoutine *r = (GenericToastRoutine *) toaster->get_vtable(PointerGetDatum(attr));
r->iterator_init(...);
r->detoast_next(...);

IX. Tests

Functional Tests

Regular regression tests are fully suitable for testing Default (Generic)
Toaster, no special tests are needed because it replaces standard TOAST mechanics.

Performance Tests

To test TOAST API overhead over master (current) TOAST implementation the
following test was used:

        /* CREATE TABLE test_toast_p (id int, jb jsonb); */
        select pg_column_size(jb ) into t_size FROM test_toast_p WHERE id = k;
		StartTime := clock_timestamp();
        /* One of the three following queries was ised in separate cycle, to
         * test INSERT, UPDATE and SELECT operations execution time, on master
         * and patched versions */
        INSERT into test_toast_p values (k,  repeat('a', i*j));
        UPDATE test_toast_p set jb = repeat('b', pg_column_size(jb)+1) where id=k;
        PERFORM jb from test_toast_p where id=k;

        EndTime := clock_timestamp();
        Delta := 1000 * ( extract(epoch from EndTime) - extract(epoch from StartTime) );

Tests show that TOAST API does not have any overhead in means of performance over
standard implementation.

X. Audit note: cleaned-tree minimization

This section records the rationale for the API surface as it stands
after commit 3ab8a37 ("Minimize Toast API surface to what cleaned
jsonb toaster needs").

X.0 Scope principle for the Toast API

The Toast API is a lifecycle interface, not an operator interface.
It exists so that a toaster can manage the lifecycle of a TOASTed
value: turn an in-memory Datum into stored representation, retrieve
it back, ask its size, propagate it across copies, replace it on
update, and dispose of it on delete or rollback.  The cleaned
surface is exactly:

    tsr_toast
    tsr_detoast
    Toastapi_size_hook        (size; queried by core via cheap hook)
    tsr_delete
    tsr_copy
    tsr_update
    tsr_validate

Plus a single narrow read-path bridge that core jsonb operator code
uses to skip detoast when reading one field out of a custom jsonb
varlena:

    Toastapi_jsonb_object_field_hook

Anything beyond lifecycle should not accumulate here.  In particular,
per-type operator fast paths (bytea `||`, jsonb_path_exists,
text concat, etc.) do NOT belong on the Toast API surface even
though they could be wired the same way.  A type's specialised
read or update fast path is a property of the type, not of the
toaster contract.  If a type wants such a fast path, the right
shape is a type-local provider interface on the type itself
(for example a bytea-provider interface registered by the type's
operators), not a new Toastapi_*_hook.  The
Toastapi_jsonb_object_field_hook is grandfathered as the single
existing read-side bridge; it is not a template.

X.1 init

Removed.  The original tsr_init was called from
CheckAndCreateToastTable and BootstrapToastTable, once per toaster
per relation.  The only shipped consumer (jsonb_toaster_init) was
a one-line passthrough to vanilla create_toast_table:

    static void
    jsonb_toaster_init(Relation rel, Oid toastoid, ...)
    {
        (void) create_toast_table(rel, toastoid, ...);
    }

The cleaned core (src/backend/catalog/toasting.c) calls
create_toast_table directly for every column regardless of
atttoaster, which is uniform and correct for every toaster the
cleaned line ships.  Removing tsr_init eliminates a level of
indirection that no current toaster exercises.

A future toaster that needs to skip toast-table creation or create
additional relations at create time would have to arrange setup
via SQL on extension install (which is what jsonb_toaster_lite
does for jbtl_subtree_refs) or via a private _PG_init.

X.2 get_vtable

Removed.  The original get_vtable was the integration point that
let a custom TOAST pointer participate in vanilla operator code
paths without forcing detoast.  Core consumers were:

  - src/backend/utils/adt/json_generic.c:DatumGetJson -- generic
    JsonContainerOps polymorphism, picking the toaster's own
    container_ops if the magic matched JSON_TOASTER_MAGIC.
  - src/backend/utils/adt/varlena.c:byteacat -- appendable bytea
    fast path, picking the toaster's append() function if the
    magic matched BYTEA_TOASTER_MAGIC.

For jsonb/JBTL the cleaned line does NOT keep the old generic
vtable/container-ops model.  The bridge from core jsonb code to a
JBTL value is the single narrow hook
Toastapi_jsonb_object_field_hook on the jsonb_object_field
operator.  Cleaned M7 (DIFF read fast path) rides on that hook.
The generic vtable was too broad for the cleaned scope.

For bytea the cleaned line does NOT have an equivalent in any
form.  Cleaned core byteacat (src/backend/utils/adt/bytea.c) is
plain vanilla -- no VARATT_IS_CUSTOM dispatch.  The
appendable-bytea fast path from the 2022 "One TOAST fits all"
talk is not reachable in cleaned.  Direct INSERT/UPDATE through
tsr_toast/tsr_update still produces appendable storage, but the
`||` operator falls back to full bytea_catenate.

Do NOT add Toastapi_bytea_append_hook.

A bytea-specific operator fast path does not belong on the
general Toast API surface (see X.0).  If appendable bytea is ever
reopened, the right shape is a type-local bytea provider
interface that the type's operators can probe -- a property of
the bytea type, not of the toaster contract.  Mechanically that
keeps the Toast API focused on lifecycle and lets each type
evolve its own specialised contract without enlarging the
common interface.

X.3 relinfo

Removed.  The original tsr_relinfo was wired in
src/backend/commands/vacuum.c at the VACUUM FULL prep step.  If
any column's toaster returned TOASTREL_VACUUM_FULL_DISABLED,
VACUUM FULL was skipped (with a WARNING) for the table.  The
only shipped consumer (jsonb_toaster_relinfo) returned exactly
that bit unconditionally, disabling VACUUM FULL on any table
managed by old jsonb_toaster.

Historical correction.  The 3ab8a37 commit message described
relinfo as having "no consumer in tree".  That is true for the
cleaned set of toasters (no current cleaned toaster uses
VACUUM-FULL disable), but the vacuum.c wiring itself was a real
consumer in core, and the old jsonb_toaster actively used the
hook.  The historical record should reflect that: relinfo was
not meaningless; it was unnecessary for the cleaned scope.

Removing relinfo is currently safe because the cleaned storage
modes (JBTL_POINTER, JBTL_POINTER_COMPRESSED_CHUNKS,
JBTL_POINTER_DIFF, JBTL_POINTER_DIFF_COMP, JBTL_POINTER_SUBTREE)
do not share chunk rows across heap-tuple versions at chunk-row
granularity.  VACUUM FULL on a JBTL-managed table re-toasts each
live heap tuple through tsr_toast and produces fresh chains; the
storage shape may normalize across the rewrite but the logical
value is preserved.  Regression coverage for this property lives
alongside the update-side v1 tests.

If a future cleaned mode reintroduces chunk-row sharing across
heap-tuple versions (for example, M-C-style array chunk-TID
inline arrays -- see M9_FORENSIC.md), VACUUM FULL is no longer
automatically safe and the new mode must come with an explicit
story.  Three shapes are conceivable; the choice is the
new-mode designer's, not the Toast API's:

  (a) re-introduce a relinfo-like disable so VACUUM FULL is
      refused on tables using that mode;
  (b) prove and implement that the rewrite path correctly
      materializes shared chunks under the caller's snapshot;
  (c) supply a rewrite-time mapping from old chunk TIDs to new
      chunk TIDs that the rewrite path consults.

This audit note records the invariant: the safety of removing
relinfo is conditional on the current storage modes only.
